I’ve fixed the hypothesis before training: difficulty is the source-normalized rank of lexical confusability—query overlap with the negative minus overlap with the positive—with query length used only to break exact ties. Holding every bucket’s exposure count and HQ/VL composition constant, an easy→hard order should outperform the exact hard→easy reversal because low-confusability triplets establish the retrieval geometry during warmup, while confusable negatives receive the full learning rate later. This predicts higher `R` without lower `G`; the reversal is the falsifying control.
