+++ b/policy/HYPOTHESIS.md
@@ -0,0 +1,20 @@
+# Lexical-confusability curriculum
+
+## Falsifiable hypothesis
+
+Within each immutable source, rank triplets by lexical confusability: cosine-normalized
+set overlap between the query and negative minus the corresponding query-positive
+overlap. Query token [REDACTED] and row identity deterministically break exact ties. Split
+each source into equal-sized quintiles so every phase preserves the fixed HQ/VL mix.
+
+With exactly 200 updates and 12,800 attempted exposures from every quintile, the
+easy-to-hard order should improve the three-subject reasoning aggregate without
+regressing the four-dataset general aggregate relative to the hard-to-easy reversal.
+The proposed mechanism is that low-confusability triplets establish broad retrieval
+geometry during optimizer warmup, after which full-rate updates from confusable
+negatives sharpen distinctions. A lower reasoning score or general-retrieval
+regression relative to the reversal falsifies this ordering claim.
+
+The two policies differ only in quintile order; source membership, rows, per-bucket
+exposure counts, trainer, model, objective, seed, optimizer, and total budget are held
+fixed.
