diff --git a/trials/RESULTS.md b/trials/RESULTS.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..cfceb214371a23dd845d198b21f800239ef6a468
--- /dev/null
+++ b/trials/RESULTS.md
@@ -0,0 +1,86 @@
+# Difficulty-order curriculum results
+
+The immutable pool was scored by source-normalized lexical confusability: normalized
+query-negative token [REDACTED] minus normalized query-positive overlap, with query length
+and row identity as deterministic tie breakers. Seven rows with an empty member of the
+triplet were excluded from every score-based policy. The paired order policies used
+exactly 12,800 exposures from each of five quintiles. Every scoreable trial used
+64,000 exposures and 1,000 optimizer updates.
+
+| Policy | R | delta R vs baseline | G | delta G vs baseline | Non-regression | Reward |
+| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- | ---: |
+| easy-to-hard | 0.20020 | -0.01121 | 0.36365 | +0.00239 | yes | 0.20020 |
+| hard-to-easy | 0.21536 | +0.00395 | 0.36313 | +0.00187 | yes | 0.21536 |
+| hard-anchor, medium-finish | 0.22273 | +0.01132 | 0.35000 | -0.01126 | no | -0.01126 |
+| hard-anchor, low/medium replay | 0.21689 | +0.00548 | 0.35758 | -0.00368 | no | -0.00368 |
+| safe uniform | 0.21811 | +0.00670 | 0.36395 | +0.00269 | yes | 0.21811 |
+| uniform, easiest quintile filtered | 0.21260 | +0.00119 | 0.36215 | +0.00089 | yes | 0.21260 |
+| hard warmup, then uniform | 0.21794 | +0.00653 | 0.36204 | +0.00078 | yes | 0.21794 |
+| static `exp(difficulty)` weighting | 0.20985 | -0.00156 | 0.34754 | -0.01372 | no | -0.01372 |
+
+The pinned protocol baseline implied by the reported deltas is R0=0.21141 and
+G0=0.36126. Hard-to-easy improves R by 0.01516 over its matched reverse while giving
+up only 0.00052 G; both policies pass general-retrieval non-regression.
+
+| Dataset | Easy-to-hard | Hard-to-easy | Hard minus easy |
+| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
+| Biology | 0.25773 | 0.26178 | +0.00405 |
+| Pony | 0.06070 | 0.11395 | +0.05325 |
+| TheoremQA-theorems | 0.28216 | 0.27034 | -0.01182 |
+| NFCorpus | 0.21503 | 0.18969 | -0.02534 |
+| SciFact | 0.63312 | 0.61987 | -0.01325 |
+| FiQA-2018 | 0.16791 | 0.14463 | -0.02328 |
+| ArguAna | 0.43854 | 0.49832 | +0.05978 |
+
+The preregistered claim that easy examples should establish retrieval geometry before
+late hard-negative sharpening is rejected. The training loss separated the score
+strongly (near zero on the easiest quintile versus roughly 0.5-1.5 on the two hardest),
+so this is not a failure of the score to order optimizer difficulty. The matched
+evaluation is instead consistent with a hard-first mechanism: exposing confusable
+negatives early, including at reduced warmup learning rates, establishes useful
+discriminative directions, while the later easier examples consolidate them without
+the late high-loss displacement seen under easy-to-hard. This mechanism interpretation
+is limited to this fixed pool, score, seed policy, and two matched orders.
+
+An earlier easy-to-hard attempt failed at admitted update 566 on an empty VL negative.
+It is retained only as a safety diagnostic and is excluded from the policy comparison.
+
+The third policy retained the first 400 hard-to-easy updates, inserted the easiest
+bucket in the middle, and ended at median difficulty. It improved all three-subject R
+to 0.22273, but reduced every BEIR metric relative to hard-to-easy and failed the
+general non-regression constraint. This supports a terminal-displacement interpretation:
+the median-only finish is useful for reasoning but too concentrated to preserve broad
+retrieval. A fourth targeted test keeps the first 800 updates identical and replaces
+the final median-only phase with uniform replay over buckets 0 through 2.
+
+The replay follow-up recovered 0.00758 G relative to median-only finishing while
+retaining R above the hard-to-easy result, but G=0.35758 remained 0.00368 below the
+non-regression floor. This corroborates terminal replay as a partial regularizer, but
+does not produce a scoreable improvement. Further neighboring replay ratios would be
+a routine mixture sweep, so the retained policy is the completed hard-to-easy order.
+
+The safety-matched uniform control then falsified the remaining order claim. It
+outperformed hard-to-easy by 0.00275 R and 0.00082 G. Sequential hard-to-easy remained
+better on Pony and ArguAna, but uniform was better on Biology, TheoremQA, NFCorpus,
+SciFact, and FiQA. The mechanism evidence favors continuous interleaving over temporal
+curriculum order for aggregate transfer.
+
+The final filtering test removed bucket 0 and continuously interleaved buckets 1
+through 4. It preserved general-retrieval non-regression, but reduced R by 0.00551 and
+G by 0.00180 relative to safe uniform. The near-zero-loss easiest quintile is therefore
+not wasted capacity under this fixed budget: retaining it appears to regularize or
+anchor transferable retrieval geometry. The best valid policy remains safe uniform.
+
+The optimizer-aligned hybrid used bucket 4 only for the fixed 60-step linear warmup,
+then replayed all five buckets uniformly for 940 updates. It improved Pony by 0.00545
+relative to safe uniform, but reduced Biology by 0.00296, TheoremQA by 0.00299, and all
+four BEIR metrics. Aggregate R fell by 0.00017 and G by 0.00191. This falsifies the
+prediction that hard-only direction setting during warmup adds to the uniform policy;
+even this brief temporal concentration causes a small aggregate transfer cost.
+
+Static `exp(difficulty)` sampling kept all quintiles continuously active but shifted
+52.0% of exposures into the two hardest quintiles. It reduced R by 0.00826 and G by
+0.01641 relative to safe uniform; every BEIR metric declined. Thus preserving easy
+rows is insufficient when their sampling mass is reduced. The exact `exp(-difficulty)`
+reverse is required before attributing the result to weighting direction rather than
+any departure from uniformity.
